 "literal": "Hunting red dog scratched-and-eaten leftover / wild-jasmine swarmed muddy small-water / bangle-possessing handed-she with-us to-drink / may-she-come indeed, ah, herself! / Is she to be pitied? She is tender, she who resided in my heart!",
 "translation": "May she, who wears bangles on her hands, come with us to drink the tiny amount of muddy water swarmed by wild jasmine, left over after a hunting red dog scratched and ate! How tender she is, she who has taken her abode in my heart!",
 "modern": "வேட்டையாடும் செந்நாய் தோண்டித் தின்றதன் மிச்சமாக உள்ளதும், காட்டு மல்லிகை மொய்க்கின்ற அழுகிய சேறு கலந்த சிறிய அளவு நீரை, வளையல் அணிந்த கைகளை உடைய என் தலைவி என்னோடு சேர்ந்து குடிப்பதற்காக வரட்டும். என் நெஞ்சில் அமர்ந்திருக்கும் அவள் எவ்வளவு எளியவள்! எவ்வளவு அன்பிற்குரியவள்!",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the arid wasteland (pālai) landscape, spoken by the hero who is eloping with his beloved. As they journey through the harsh desert, they find only a tiny puddle of muddy water, which has been scratched up by a wild red dog and is covered with fallen wild jasmine flowers. Despite the poor quality and scarcity of the water, the hero wishes for his delicate, bangle-clad beloved to share this meager drink with him. He marvels at her tenderness and willingness to endure such hardships for his sake, expressing his deep love and concern for her. The contrast between her delicate nature ('bangle-wearing hands') and the harsh reality of the wasteland ('muddy water left by a wild dog') highlights the intensity of her love and his protective affection.",
